.\" The `mc mgmt` subcommand. Named mc-mgmt.1 because `mc man mgmt` resolves a
.\" plugin command to mc-<plugin name>(1) — see mc-plugins(5).
.TH MC-MGMT 1 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-mgmt \- management-protocol console and graceful shutdown for mc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mc mgmt status
.br
.B mc mgmt players
.br
.B mc mgmt say
.I TEXT
.br
.B mc mgmt enable
.br
.B mc mgmt disable
.br
.B mc mgmt allowlist
.RI [ "add \fR| \fPremove PLAYER" ]
.br
.B mc mgmt bans
.RI [ "add \fR| \fPremove PLAYER" ]
.br
.B mc mgmt ip\-bans
.RI [ "add \fR| \fPremove ADDRESS" ]
.br
.B mc mgmt operators
.RI [ "add \fR| \fPremove PLAYER" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mc-mgmt
package adds the
.B mc mgmt
subcommand to
.BR mc (1),
and the lifecycle hooks that make a stop graceful and a backup consistent. It
speaks the
.IR "Minecraft Server Management Protocol" :
JSON\-RPC 2.0 carried over a WebSocket, served by the game itself from Minecraft
.B 1.21.9
onwards.
.PP
It is a console in the sense
.BR mc-plugins (5)
means it \(em something that can talk to a server that is already running \(em
and where it works it is the better one. Authentication is a bearer secret
rather than a password every client shares, and the interface is typed methods
returning typed results rather than command strings whose replies have to be
parsed out of prose. A player list arrives as a list; a player name is sent as
data, so there is no command string for a hostile name to break out of.
.PP
The protocol does not exist on older servers, and this plugin does not pretend
otherwise. On anything below 1.21.9 it stands aside and
.BR mc-rcon (1)
does the work \(em see
.B THE ELECTION
below.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mc mgmt status
Report whether the management protocol is enabled, which endpoint it resolved,
whether a connection and the bearer handshake actually succeed, and what the
server says it is running. Reads only files the
.B minecraft
group can already read, so it needs no root.
.IP
This is the command to run when a console behaves unexpectedly: it distinguishes
"the protocol is off", "the endpoint refuses connections" and "the secret is
wrong", which the election collapses into a single silent "no".
.TP
.B mc mgmt players
Print who is online, one name per line, on standard output so it can be piped.
The list arrives as a list: there is no sentence to parse and no wording a
server fork can vary. Needs root, or membership of the
.B minecraft
group.
.TP
.B mc mgmt say \fITEXT\fR
Broadcast a message to everyone online, in chat rather than the action bar,
which fades. The text is sent as a message field, not interpolated into a
command. Needs root, or membership of the
.B minecraft
group.
.TP
.B mc mgmt enable
Provision a secret if there is not one, pin a port, switch the management server
on in
.I server.properties
with TLS off on loopback, and report the endpoint. Needs root.
.IP
The server is
.I not
restarted for you. It reads
.I server.properties
at startup, so a restart is required to apply the change \(em but on a populated
server that is a five\-minute countdown, and when to spend it is the operator's
call, not a side effect of a config command. Apply it with
.BR "mc restart" .
.TP
.B mc mgmt disable
Turn the management server off. The secret is left in place, so a later
.B enable
restores the same credential rather than invalidating every client that holds
it. Needs root.
.TP
.B mc mgmt allowlist \fR[\fBadd\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIPLAYER\fR]
List the allowlist, or add and remove a player. Needs root to change it.
.TP
.B mc mgmt bans \fR[\fBadd\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIPLAYER\fR]
List player bans, or add and remove one. Needs root to change it.
.TP
.B mc mgmt ip\-bans \fR[\fBadd\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
List address bans, or add and remove one. Needs root to change it.
.TP
.B mc mgmt operators \fR[\fBadd\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIPLAYER\fR]
List operators, or grant and revoke. Needs root to change it.
.PP
Moderation is capability mc has never had over RCON, and it is here because the
protocol makes it safe to offer: each of these takes a player as a structured
value, so a name containing a quote, a section sign or a newline is a name and
not a second command.
.PP
Game rules and the server settings the protocol also exposes are deliberately
absent. Game rules are exactly where the protocol has broken compatibility
between versions, and covering them means branching on a protocol version rather
than calling a method.
.SH "THE ELECTION"
.B mc-mgmt
and
.BR mc-rcon (1)
are both console providers, and installing both is the intended arrangement.
Core does not choose between them by name. Each declares a
.B priority
in its manifest \(em RCON 10, this plugin 20 \(em and core elects the
highest\-priority console that answers
.B "<bin> console probe"
with exit 0 inside three seconds. Anything else, including a server too old to
serve the endpoint, is "no", and the next console down takes over.
.PP
Only the elected console runs
.BR pre-stop ", " pre-backup " and " post-backup ,
because two countdowns are worse than one and a second
.B save\-on
can restore saving halfway through the other console's archive. Provisioning
.RB ( post-install ", " post-upgrade )
goes to every installed console, so losing an election never leaves a machine
with nothing that works.
.PP
.B mc plugins
prints which console won:
.PP
.RS
.nf
console:  mgmt (priority 20) \(em elected
console:  rcon (priority 10) \(em standing down
.fi
.RE
.PP
A fleet of mixed Minecraft versions therefore needs no per\-host configuration:
the same two packages on every machine elect the management protocol where the
server offers it and RCON everywhere else. See
.BR mc-plugins (5).
.SH HOOKS
All of these are dispatched by core.
.BR pre-stop ", " pre-backup " and " post-backup
are subject to the election above;
.BR post-install " and " post-upgrade
run for every installed console.
.TP
.B pre-stop
Stopping warns players. With at least one player online, or when the count
cannot be determined, the stop is announced 5 minutes, 3 minutes and 1 minute
before it happens. A provably empty server stops immediately.
.IP
The unit's
.B TimeoutStopSec=
is derived from that schedule plus the probe budget, and is asserted by a test.
Re\-tiering the countdown without recomputing the value means a SIGKILL through
the JVM's chunk flush.
.TP
.BR pre-backup ", " post-backup
Backups are consistent. Autosave is switched off and the world is written to
disk and waited for, then autosave is turned back on \(em whether or not the
archive succeeded.
.TP
.BR post-install ", " post-upgrade
The management endpoint is provisioned for a newly installed server, with a
generated secret. This runs even when RCON is the elected console, so the
machine is ready the day it is upgraded to a version that serves the protocol.
.SH "PROTOCOL VERSIONS"
The protocol is young and has changed shape. What mc uses is the part that has
been stable throughout; this list is here so an operator reading a server
changelog knows which entries matter.
.TP
.B 25w35a (1.21.9)
Introduced. The management server, JSON\-RPC 2.0 over a WebSocket, and the
methods mc's console parity rests on:
.BR players ", " system_message ", " save ", " autosave ", " stop " and " status .
This is the floor;
.B mc mgmt
requires 1.21.9 or newer and stands aside on anything older.
.TP
.B 25w37a
Authentication by bearer secret, and TLS on by default. mc reads the secret
from
.B management-server-secret
and sends it in an
.B Authorization: Bearer
header; because the endpoint it configures is loopback, it also sets
.B management-server-tls-enabled=false
and connects over
.BR ws:// .
An absent key means TLS is
.IR on ,
so the scheme is assumed to be
.B wss://
unless the server says otherwise.
.TP
.B "1.21.9 pre-release 1"
The prefix on notification method names settled. mc issues requests and reads
their results rather than subscribing, so this is inert here.
.TP
.B 25w41a
.B server/activity
added.
.TP
.B 25w42a
.B management-server-allowed-origins
added \(em an origin allowlist for the WebSocket handshake. It matters only when
the endpoint is reached through a browser or a reverse proxy; mc's own client
sends no
.B Origin
header.
.TP
.B 25w44a
Game rule value types changed incompatibly. This is the break that keeps game
rules out of
.BR "mc mgmt" :
nothing mc calls is affected, and adding them would mean branching on a protocol
version.
.TP
.B "26.2 snapshot 5"
The management server starts
.I before
the game server. A probe can therefore succeed while the world is still loading,
which is a reason to read
.B mc mgmt status
rather than treat a successful connection as "the server is up".
.TP
.B "26.3 snapshot 1"
World upgrade notifications added.
.SH SECURITY
The endpoint is authenticated by a
.B 40\-character
bearer secret held in
.B management-server-secret
in
.IR /opt/minecraft/server.properties ,
which is
.B minecraft:minecraft 0640
\(em readable by the service account and the
.B minecraft
group and nobody else. The secret is never passed on a command line:
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
is world\-readable and would expose it to every local user for the lifetime of
the process.
.PP
.B mc
configures a loopback endpoint with TLS off. On loopback there is no network
path to intercept, and a self\-signed certificate would only add a trust store to
maintain. That is a deployment choice rather than a limit of the protocol: the
endpoint may be bound elsewhere and put behind a TLS\-terminating reverse proxy
for other clients, in which case set
.B management-server-allowed-origins
so that a browser on another site cannot make a viewer's own credentials open a
WebSocket to it. Unlike RCON, a non\-loopback endpoint here is reported rather
than refused \(em there is a real credential on the connection.
.PP
There is no command\-string injection surface. Every call is a method name and
typed parameters, so a player name, a ban reason or a broadcast is data at every
layer; nothing mc sends is assembled by concatenation, and nothing it reads back
has to be parsed out of prose.
.PP
Core still owns the managed keys of
.IR server.properties .
A modpack or any other writer of that file cannot switch the management server
on with a secret of its own choosing: the keys are re\-applied after any merge.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/minecraft/server.properties
Holds
.BR management-server-enabled ,
.BR management-server-host ,
.BR management-server-port ,
.B management-server-secret
and
.BR management-server-tls-enabled .
.BR "minecraft:minecraft 0640" .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/mgmt.toml
The manifest, including the console
.B priority
that decides the election.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/mc/mc-mgmt
The plugin executable. Invoked by mc, not by you.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR mc-rcon (1),
.BR mc-plugins (5),
.BR mc-config (5)
